\chapter{Introduction}

\texttt{yahoo-finance} is a Common Lisp client for Yahoo Finance chart
data.  It is \emph{not} an official Yahoo product.  Yahoo has not offered a
supported public Finance \textsc{Api} since 2017~\cite{yahoo-api-2017}.
This library talks to the unofficial \textsc{Json} chart endpoint that
powers \url{https://finance.yahoo.com}:

\begin{center}
\url{https://query2.finance.yahoo.com/v8/finance/chart/AAPL}
\end{center}

It is also not Paul Nathan's archived
\texttt{cl-yahoo-finance}~\cite{cl-yahoo-finance}, which called the old
ichart \textsc{Csv} and \textsc{Yql} surfaces.  Those URLs are gone.  This
library starts from scratch against v8 chart, the same way
\texttt{cl-coingecko}~\cite{cl-coingecko} wraps CoinGecko.

The thin layer lives in \texttt{yahoo-finance/rest-api}: \textsc{Http}, a
browser \texttt{User-Agent} (Yahoo returns 429 without one), 429 retries,
and \texttt{get-chart}.  The \texttt{yahoo-finance} package re-exports that
layer and adds the helpers you actually want: universal times,
\texttt{ohlc-history} as a list of \texttt{ohlc-bar} objects, a translator
\texttt{ohlc-bars-for-ingest} for candlesticks, and a meta plist (currency,
exchange, instrument type).

This library does \emph{not} depend on
\texttt{candlesticks}~\cite{candlesticks}.  Candlesticks is a Postgre\textsc{Sql}
store.  An application such as Livermore or Limbic loads both and calls
\texttt{ingest-candlesticks}.

v0.1 wraps only the chart endpoint.  Quote, quoteSummary, options, and
search need Yahoo's cookie/crumb dance and are out of scope.

You can download the library from GitHub at:\\
\url{https://github.com/cgore/yahoo-finance}

\section*{Getting Lisp}

Before using this library you need a working Lisp.  I use and recommend
\textsc{Sbcl}, Steel Bank Common Lisp, which is available at:\\
\url{http://www.sbcl.org}

If you are using Debian or a similar Linux distribution (including Ubuntu), you
can just run as root:\\
\texttt{apt-get install sbcl sbcl-doc sbcl-source}

\section*{Using the Library}

Clone the repository next to your other local systems, or symlink the
\textsc{Asdf} file into a directory on \path{asdf:*central-registry*}
(I use \path{~/programming/lisp/systems/}).

\begin{lstlisting}
mkdir -p ~/programming/lisp
cd ~/programming/lisp
git clone git@github.com:cgore/yahoo-finance.git
ln -s ~/programming/lisp/yahoo-finance/yahoo-finance.asd \
      ~/programming/lisp/systems/
\end{lstlisting}

The system depends on Dexador, \texttt{function-cache}, Quri, $\Sigma$, and
Yason.  Those come from Quicklisp.

\begin{lstlisting}
(ql:quickload :yahoo-finance)
(in-package :yahoo-finance)

(multiple-value-bind (bars meta)
    (ohlc-history "AAPL" :range "1mo")
  (list (length bars)
        (getf meta :currency)
        (getf meta :full-exchange-name)
        (first bars)))
\end{lstlisting}

\texttt{ohlc-history} defaults to a full daily history via
\texttt{period1}/\texttt{period2}.  Do not send \texttt{range=max} with
\texttt{interval=1d} to the raw endpoint: Yahoo downsamples that pair to
quarterly bars.  \texttt{ohlc-history} rewrites it.

\section*{Testing}

Specs are \texttt{sigma/behave} \texttt{behavior} / \texttt{should} forms
and run at load time.  \texttt{asdf:test-system} reloads the sources so
those assertions run again.  They do not hit the network; a checked-in
five-day \texttt{AAPL} fixture covers parsing.

\begin{lstlisting}
(asdf:test-system :yahoo-finance)
\end{lstlisting}

To also call Yahoo, set \texttt{YAHOO\_FINANCE\_LIVE\_TESTS=1} and run
\texttt{asdf:test-system} again.  The live suite uses a short window.
\textsc{Http} 429 is treated as a skip.

Have fun!
